Erythema nodosum
Rheumatology & Immunology
Tender anterior shin nodules, Bruise-like evolution, Accompanying arthralgias

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-014
Reason for Visit: Acute eruption of exquisitely painful, red nodules on both lower legs, accompanied by severe ankle pain, rendering the patient unable to walk or sleep due to the severe discomfort.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed acute, severe lower extremity pain with spreading, hot, red nodules. An urgent after-hours physical assessment was required to definitively rule out bilateral deep vein thrombosis, severe bilateral cellulitis, or cutaneous vasculitis, and to provide immediate, safe analgesia, as waiting until the next daytime appointment caused unmanageable distress and functional immobilization.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My shins have broken out in these massive, hot red lumps that hurt so much I can't even wear long pants, and my ankles are throbbing."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 1-week history of preceding low-grade fever and profound malaise. Over the last 72 hours, there has been an acute eruption of exquisitely tender, erythematous nodules typically located on the bilateral anterior shins. The nodules have a symmetrical, bilateral distribution. The patient notes that older lesions are evolving into bruise-like lesions. Denies any ulceration, trauma, or recent new medications. Concurrently, the patient reports severe accompanying arthralgias, most commonly in the ankles and knees, making weight-bearing agonizing.
Functional Status: Housebound, mobilizing with a severe limp due to ankle arthralgia and mechanical tenderness of the shins.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe pain limiting activities of daily living; unable to stand to cook or clean.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 mmHg | HR: 90 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient is seated with legs elevated, clearly uncomfortable and wincing when the trousers brush against the shins.
Physical Exam: Dermatological: Multiple (5-8 per leg), exquisitely tender, poorly demarcated, erythematous, warm, subcutaneous nodules (ranging from 2-5 cm in diameter) palpated firmly on the anterior aspect of bilateral lower legs. No fluctuance, no purulence, and a complete absence of overlying ulceration. Older lesions exhibit a contusiform (bruise-like) yellowish-purple hue. Musculoskeletal: Bilateral ankle joints are mildly swollen with significant periarticular tenderness and restricted range of motion secondary to pain. Mild lower extremity edema is present distally. Cardiovascular: Calves are soft, non-tender posteriorly; Homan's sign negative bilaterally. Diagnostic tetrad completely fulfilled: tender anterior shin nodules, bruise-like evolution of older lesions, and accompanying severe ankle arthralgias.
Environmental Observations: Clean living space; patient advised to clear floor clutter to avoid accidental bumping of the shins.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking paracetamol with inadequate relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Erythema nodosum - Status: Exacerbated (Acute eruptive phase)
Differentials:

Bilateral Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The clearly nodular, discrete nature of the lesions (rather than confluent, spreading erythema), the bilateral symmetry, and the absence of a distinct portal of entry or high fever.

Cutaneous Vasculitis (e.g., Henoch-Schonlein) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The lesions are large, deep, tender subcutaneous nodules rather than flat or slightly raised palpable purpura/petechiae.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The swelling is anterior and nodular rather than deep calf swelling, and the presentation is bilateral and symmetrical.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severely painful eruption of classic Erythema nodosum complicated by significant ankle arthralgia. The severe mechanical pain and inability to comfortably weight-bear or sleep required an urgent after-hours bedside assessment. It was critical to physically examine the lesions to confidently exclude deep vein thrombosis and bilateral cellulitis, conditions requiring immediate emergency intervention, and to prescribe optimized high-dose NSAID therapy. Deferring this assessment until standard hours would have left the patient in severe, unmanageable pain and high anxiety regarding the diagnosis, satisfying the MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiate a regular, scheduled dosing regimen of high-dose NSAIDs (e.g., Naproxen or Ibuprofen) combined with a proton pump inhibitor, pending normal renal function. Recommend strict leg elevation. Arrange a routine GP follow-up for baseline bloods, a throat swab (to check for strep as a trigger), and a chest X-ray to assess for hilar lymphadenopathy (evaluating for underlying sarcoidosis, TB, or fungal etiologies).
Positive Behavioral Change: Strict elevation protocol: Elevate legs above the level of the heart continuously when seated; successfully observing a rapid decrease in the throbbing pain and swelling will strongly reinforce the mechanical necessity of this action. NSAID scheduling: Taking the anti-inflammatory medication strictly with meals on a set schedule, rather than waiting for pain to peak, to maintain a steady suppression of the profound inflammatory response.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 7 days for investigation of underlying triggers.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or regular GP urgently if the nodules break open and ulcerate, if you develop a severe, persistent cough, profound shortness of breath, or if a high swinging fever (>38.5 C) occurs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This rash is a reactive inflammation of the fat under the skin. While the rash itself is usually self-limiting, we must perform blood tests and an x-ray soon to ensure there is no hidden underlying infection or condition causing it.
Clinical Course: The nodules will slowly fade over 3 to 6 weeks, turning into bruise-like marks before disappearing completely without lasting scarring.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If the lumps burst open, or if you develop a severe cough or high fevers, you must seek medical review immediately as this could indicate a different, more serious type of skin infection.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system has caused severe inflammation in the fat layer of your shins. The strong anti-inflammatories will block the chemical signals causing the pain, and elevating your legs will physically drain the excess fluid pressing on your nerves.
Actionable Steps: Keep your legs elevated above your hips at all times when resting. Take the NSAIDs exactly on time with food. See your regular doctor next week for a chest x-ray.

